## 3. Q1-Q5: exact queries, moments, rank, and fibres

### Precise statements and reconstruction

Expanding the literal indicator gives

\[
Q(f;S,a)=2^{n-|S|}\sum_{T\subseteq S}(-1)^{\sum_{i\in T}a_i}\widehat f(T).
\]

Conversely,

\[
\widehat f(T)=2^{-n}\sum_{a\in\{0,1\}^T}(-1)^{\sum_{i\in T}a_i}Q(f;T,a).
\]

Thus Q_k and the Fourier coefficients of degree at most k determine each other. The factors of two are essential; an implementation cannot silently replace integer arithmetic with arithmetic modulo two.

For the integer row lattice put M_T=Q(f;T,1^T)=sum_x f(x)prod_{i in T}x_i. For disjoint positive and negative literal sets P,Z,

\[
Q(f;P\cup Z,(1_P,0_Z))=
\sum_{U\subseteq Z}(-1)^{|U|}M_{P\cup U}.
\]

The M_T rows themselves occur among the queries, so they form an integral basis of the query row lattice. With W_T=2^n fhat(T),

\[
W_T=\sum_{U\subseteq T}(-2)^{|U|}M_U,
\quad
M_T=2^{-|T|}\sum_{U\subseteq T}(-1)^{|U|}W_U.
\]

The latter change is invertible over Q; it is **not** generally unimodular over Z. The milestone's lattice statement is valid with the moment basis, not a license to identify the integer Walsh and moment lattices without divisibility constraints.

The characters indexed by |T|<=k are linearly independent. Therefore

\[
\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb Q} Q_k
=\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbb R}Q_k
=\sum_{j=0}^{k}\binom nj.
\]

For |S|<k, extend S to a k-element set U and sum Q(f;U,b) over completions b of a. Hence exactly-k queries span the same space. Finally, Q_k(f)=Q_k(g) iff the Fourier coefficients of f-g through degree k vanish. Over all real functions the kernel is the span of characters of degrees greater than k; on Boolean functions a fibre is the intersection of its affine coset with the Boolean cube. The real kernel dimension is not the logarithm of the size of every Boolean fibre.

### Attacks and edges

At n=k=0 there is one truth entry and one count. At k=n the map is injective. Omitting labels or the empty query changes the theorem. Extending 'exactly k' beyond n is invalid. A simple unordered-marginal multiset can conflate coordinate permutations, whereas the stated indexed operator does not.

