## 4. Q6-Q7: collision size and design trades

For 0<=k<n, if distinct Boolean f,g have Q_k(f)=Q_k(g), then

\[
|\{x:f(x)\ne g(x)\}|\geq 2^{k+1}.
\]

Proof reconstruction: h=f-g has no Fourier terms of degrees <=k. Multiplication by chi_[n] complements the character indices, so h chi_[n] is a nonzero real multilinear polynomial of degree at most n-k-1 with exactly the same support. A nonzero degree-d multilinear polynomial on an n-cube has support at least 2^{n-d}. One proof is induction: if a variable appears, write p=x_i q+r with nonzero q of degree at most d-1; at every point where q is nonzero at least one of the two extensions has nonzero p. The constant case starts the induction. Applying this bound gives the claimed distance.

Equality is realized by opposite parity halves on a (k+1)-dimensional coordinate subcube, both zero outside it. The two disjoint supports have equal inclusion counts for every set of at most k coordinates; this is a simple [k]-trade. Conversely, the all-positive moments plus inclusion-exclusion turn every such trade into a Q_k collision. Common ones of f and g must first be cancelled.

The k=n case has no distinct collisions and no sharp witness. For k=0, two functions with equal weight can differ at exactly two points, as the formula predicts.

The original program exhausts collision minima only through n=3, with explicit witnesses beyond that. The new audit did not conduct an all-pairs distance search at n=4; its exhaustive fibre comparison is a different check. The proof supplies the general bound.

**Priority:** S2 gives the existing minimum-volume trade theory. Minimum trade volume is 2^k per leg; the corresponding Boolean disagreement is twice that, 2^{k+1}. The factor two must not be lost. The bridge is useful synthesis, not a new distance theorem.

## 5. Q8-Q12: class reconstruction thresholds

Assume parameters t,d lie between 0 and n, with the explicit d>=1 qualification for Q12.

**Q8, all Boolean functions.** kappa=n. Point queries recover f, and complementary global parity have identical proper marginals.

**Q9, at-most-t juntas.** kappa=t. Each function has Fourier degree at most t, even if its active coordinates are unknown. Equality of data through t therefore gives equality. Opposite parity on a chosen t-element set supplies the lower bound; constants give t=0.

**Q10, Boolean functions of real Fourier degree at most d.** kappa=d. All nonzero Fourier information is included by depth d, and opposite d-variable parity proves sharpness.

**Q11, degree-d polynomial threshold functions.** kappa=d under a declared threshold convention. Let f be represented by a polynomial p of degree <=d with sign agreeing with 2f-1 and no zero on the finite cube. If Boolean g has the same degree-d moments, then sum_x p(x)(f(x)-g(x))=0. Every nonzero summand is strictly positive, so g=f. A consistent tie convention can be perturbed on the finite cube to obtain a strict representation. Opposite parity on d variables gives the lower bound. This is generalized Chow uniqueness; it does not give a fast inversion algorithm by itself. S3 states and strengthens the known theorem; S4 is an earlier primary antecedent.

**Q12, GF(2) ANF degree at most d, d>=1.** kappa=n because the class already contains global affine parity and its complement. GF(2) polynomial degree and real Fourier degree are different parameters. For d=0 the class consists of constants and kappa=0; omitting this exception is wrong.

The proof mechanisms are standard or immediate consequences. Direct enumeration is not required to establish them; the submitted evidence correctly relies on proofs here. The 2026 random-function work S5 concerns typical functions and does not contradict these worst-case parity bounds. Neither its threshold nor its claimed novelty can be imported into this manuscript as a rank-class result.

## 6. Q13-Q14: rank-one/rank-two jump

### Contract

Fix disjoint nonempty coordinate blocks A,B, |A|=p, |B|=q. Every truth table is flattened into a 2^p by 2^q binary matrix, using this same named cut. Let C_r be the full class of matrices of GF(2) rank at most r, including zero. Class membership is promised. There is no optimization over cuts in the theorem.

### Rank one: independent proof

A nonzero rank-one binary matrix over GF(2) is u v^T for nonzero Boolean vectors u,v. Set w_u=sum u, w_v=sum v. Its row sums equal w_v u, and its column sums equal w_u v. Fixing all A variables obtains every row sum; fixing all B variables obtains every column sum. Data through max(p,q) therefore recover the supports of u and v and hence M. A zero total count identifies the zero matrix.

For the lower bound assume p>=q. Choose complementary parity functions u and 1-u on all p A variables and let v be identically one. Both matrices have rank one. A restriction of fewer than p total variables leaves at least one A variable free, so the conditioned counts coincide. Thus kappa(C_1)=max(p,q).

### Rank at least two: independent proof

Let a and b denote parities of the A and B variables. For odd global parity,

\[
M=a(\mathbf 1-b)^T+(\mathbf 1-a)b^T,
\]

where the two rectangles are disjoint. For even global parity use a b^T+(1-a)(1-b)^T. Across a nonempty cut both have rank exactly two over GF(2). They have equal conditioned counts whenever fewer than p+q variables are fixed. They differ on every complete assignment. Consequently kappa(C_r)=p+q for every r>=2.

This is an exact worst-case statement. It is not a reconstruction-time or storage lower bound. If r=0, the class has one member and kappa=0. Empty blocks are outside the stated jump; rank two cannot occur there. The upper bound remains n even if the declared r exceeds maximum feasible matrix rank.

### Additional hostile comparison

The two witnesses also have ordinary real rank two, nonnegative rank two, and Boolean-OR rank two. Rank-one binary matrices in these senses are the same rectangles. Thus the same threshold jump holds for the corresponding binary rank-bounded classes. This is an audit-derived observation with the proof above, not a claim that its exact sentence has been independently published. It weakens positioning as a uniquely GF(2) phenomenon.

### Direct computation now available

All truth functions were examined for cuts (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (1,3), (2,2), (3,1), filtering separately to rank <=1 and <=2. For the 4x4 cut, class sizes are 226 and 7,576. At k=3 the latter has 7,575 fibres, and at k=4 it has 7,576. Complete rank-one classes for cuts (2,3) and (3,3), of sizes 3,826 and 65,026, respectively, also attain threshold 3.

These new checks directly address a gap in the original Theory verifier. They do not change the short proof into a stronger theorem, and they must be credited as audit-added evidence rather than retroactively attributed to the new Computation thread.

### Novelty assessment

No inspected primary source was found asserting this exact kappa formula. The constituent rectangle characterization, row/column marginal recovery, and parity obstruction are elementary or standard; the former is also already in the companion LM paper. Classify as **CORRECT BUT MINOR, PRIORITY UNCERTAIN**. It is a plausible concise corollary, not yet a substantial theory-first paper on its own.

## 7. Q15-Q17: persistent information

### Q15: real-linear sketch theorem survives

Suppose L:R^(2^n)->R^m is linear and some arbitrary decoder D satisfies Q_k(f)=D(Lf) for **every real-valued f**. For any v in ker L, L(f+v)=Lf, hence Q_k(v)=0. Therefore ker L is contained in ker Q_k, and rank L >= rank Q_k = sum_{j<=k}binom(n,j). Low-degree moments attain this dimension.

No linearity of D is needed. The domain quantifier is crucial. On the finite Boolean domain, the one-dimensional linear map f -> sum_{i=0}^{2^n-1}2^i f_i is injective and admits a nonlinear decoder; its output uses up to 2^n bits. Thus scalar dimension and bit complexity cannot be exchanged. The milestone itself states the real-domain limitation; retain it prominently.

For deterministic fixed-width b-bit summaries sufficient for a query family L on a finite class C, the general counting bound is

\[
b\geq\left\lceil\log_2 |L(C)|\right\rceil.
\]

If the queries include every point value, |L(C)|=|C|. That is an information bound, not a guarantee that a minimal encoding is efficiently computable or supports efficient updates.

### Q16: cumulative adaptivity, not resetting persistence

A correct precise statement is: two functions are indistinguishable by **every** adaptive coordinate-restriction protocol fixing at most t distinct coordinates in total, and returning counts along its path, iff their Q_t data agree. Every output is one Q_t component. Conversely, any Q_t component is obtainable by a nonadaptive sequence, which is a permitted special case.

One observed path does not reveal the entire Q_t family. Repeatedly assigning an already fixed coordinate does not increase the number of distinct fixed variables; incompatible assignments give zero and need a stated convention. A path-length bound and a distinct-coordinate budget should not be silently interchanged.

For a restriction rho fixing s<=k coordinates, original Q_k determines Q_{k-s}(f|rho), since the union of old and new fixed coordinates has size <=k. It does not generally determine Q_k(f|rho) with a reset horizon. Take odd and even parity on k+1 variables: their Q_k data agree, but after fixing one variable the residual k-variable full query data differ. This counterexample was checked for n=2,...,10.

Accordingly classify Q16 **CORRECT WITH REPAIR** at the contract/presentation level. Its cumulative interpretation is elementary and sound; any resetting interpretation is false.

### Q17: what remains genuinely open in this package

For M=UW over GF(2), and compatible row/column index sets I,J, let alpha_a count restricted rows of U with state a and beta_b count restricted columns of W with state b. The identity

\[
N(I,J)=\tfrac12\left(|I||J|-\sum_{a,b}\alpha_a\beta_b(-1)^{a\cdot b}\right)
\]

is already proved as Draft 3 Theorem 7.1. The new audit checks it for every restriction of every rank-at-most-two 4x4 matrix. Its sufficiency **for that selected query** is not open.

What is not supplied is a new theorem giving a minimal coordinate-aware persistent state, efficient transformation of that state under the declared restriction protocol, sharp bit bounds under an explicit model, and matching query/update complexity. Gauge changes U->UG, W->G^{-1}W for invertible G relabel states while preserving the pairing; a claimed canonical or minimal state must account for this redundancy. Unconditional histograms erase coordinate membership and fail even on f=xy versus g=(1-x)y.

**Classification:** UNPROVED for the proposed new minimality/complexity result. The mere profile identity is correct but standard and already belongs to the prior report.

## 8. Draft 3 theorem-by-theorem attack

### B1. Theorem 5.1 - family-wise conditioning

Restrict each disjoint XOR/product component on its own coordinates; restrict rank factors to compatible rows/columns; restrict cofactor selectors and prototypes and merge equal/complementary prototypes. Each operation denotes precisely the residual truth function. This follows entrywise or by substitution. Named coordinate axes and retained irrelevant-variable multiplicities are part of the contract. Empty residual universes have one assignment, not zero. **CORRECT BUT STANDARD.** Small-function exhaustive and sequential/fused tests exercise the claim.

### B2. Proposition 5.2 - monotonicity of a retained representation

Submatrix rank cannot increase; restricting existing factors need not increase their stored width; restricting existing cofactor rows cannot increase the number of used prototypes. A fixed-width factor payload r(R'+C') decreases with retained dimensions. These statements do not claim that freshly rediscovered disjoint components are monotone. Indeed xyz XOR x XOR y has one connected ANF support component, but z=0 leaves x XOR y, with two disjoint components. **CORRECT BUT MINOR**, with the retained-versus-reoptimized distinction essential.

### B3. Theorem 6.1 - exact counts for disjoint artifacts

For disjoint exhaustive blocks with zero/one counts z_i,o_i and t_i=z_i+o_i, an XOR with constant c has

\[
\#1=\frac{\prod_i t_i-(-1)^c\prod_i(z_i-o_i)}2.
\]

For conjunction/product the count is prod_i o_i. Cofactor counts are selector multiplicities times prototype weights, with complements replacing weight w by residual column volume minus w. Disjointness, declared free-variable scope, and constant handling are indispensable. The formulas are **CORRECT BUT STANDARD**; see decomposition/semiring antecedents S9-S11. No new exact-counting framework follows from these identities alone.

### B4. Theorem 7.1 - GF(2) rank-profile algorithm

The profile identity above follows from the bit equality z=(1-(-1)^z)/2. A Walsh-Hadamard transform evaluates the bilinear sum using O(r 2^r) integer additions plus a length-2^r dot product after histogram construction. Workspace is O(2^r) integers, not O(2^r) bits. Counts can require O(log(|I||J|+1)) bits. Profile construction and coordinate indexing must be charged.

An explicit row-stream method is polynomial in the explicit factor dimensions, with a straightforward O(r R' C') bit-operation bound and a retained row-sized buffer; native bitsets change the word-level constant/model. Neither algorithm is polynomial in n just because the function has n variables. **CORRECT BUT STANDARD/SYNTHESIS**. S12 supplies the transform antecedent; S11/S16 place counting in existing algebraic/tensor frameworks. The timed baseline uses its implemented grouped/row-stream paths, not a newly benchmarked transform-optimal persistence engine.

### B5. Proposition 7.2 - factor-only minimal recompression

Factor U=AB with A full column rank. Let D=BW and factor D=EF minimally. Then UW=(AE)F, and injectivity of A implies rank(UW)=rank(D), so the width is minimal. A factorization of inner width t is minimal iff both left and right factors have rank t. Cancellation matters; one factor's rank alone is not sufficient. Standard elimination gives a bound O((R'+C')r^2) in GF(2) operations for the declared input sizes. **CORRECT BUT STANDARD.** The 2,000 seeded recompressions test instances, not exhaustive dimensions.

### B6. Theorem 8.1 - complementary cofactor classes

Let W be the GF(2) row space of a nonempty binary matrix with rank r. Put delta=1 if the all-ones row is in W and 0 otherwise, and let c be the number of occurring row classes modulo complementation. Then

\[
r\leq c+\delta,\qquad
1\leq c\leq\min(R,2^{r-\delta}).
\]

If delta=0, no two elements of W are complementary; the observed rows are distinct representatives and span a space of dimension at most c. If delta=1, quotienting W by the line generated by the all-ones row has dimension r-1, and representatives together with that row span dimension at most c+1. These observations give both bounds. Zero matrices fit with r=0,delta=0,c=1. **CORRECT BUT MINOR; exact packaging PRIORITY UNCERTAIN.** It is a quotient-space count, not an optimal compressed-file bound.

### B7. Proposition 8.2 - the inner-product example

For M_r(x,y)=x dot y over GF(2), x,y in {0,1}^r, the GF(2) rank is r and all 2^r rows are different, with none complementary because every row is zero at y=0. An explicit whole-row dictionary therefore stores 2^(2r) prototype bits, whereas the displayed rank factors use 2r2^r bits. This is a correct separation of these **fixed-cut explicit payload formats**.

The ordinary real rank of the 0/1 matrix is 2^r-1; the ordinary real rank of the sign matrix (-1)^(x dot y) is 2^r. The latter is Walsh orthogonality. It must not be described as communication-complexity sign-rank. The fields and encodings differ; S8 is useful context on rank conventions.

The example is not an all-order OBDD lower bound: blocked x-then-y order has 2^r different residuals, whereas interleaving x_i,y_i permits constant-width parity accumulation and O(r) nodes. It also has an obvious disjoint XOR of pairwise products. **CORRECT BUT STANDARD/MINOR REPRESENTATION COMPARISON**, not a new optimized-KC or tensor separation. S6/S7 already demand query- and order-specific comparisons.

### B8. Proposition 9.1 - unconditional profiles fail as future state

For f=xy and g=(1-x)y, suitable rank-one factorizations have identical unconditioned state histograms and equal total count one. Restricting x=0 gives counts zero and one. Thus unordered aggregate factor-state histograms are insufficient to answer later coordinate-specific queries without extra information. **CORRECT BUT MINOR.** It is a valid counterexample, not a lower bound on every compressed coordinate-aware representation.

### B9. Proposition 9.2 - full-query storage lower bound

A fixed-width exact state for a finite function class F answering all restrictions, including full assignments, must distinguish all |F| functions; hence b>=ceil(log2 |F|). For all Boolean functions this is 2^n bits. **CORRECT BUT STANDARD.** It says nothing like 'every low-rank function needs a full truth table'; the class cardinality and allowed variable metadata must remain explicit.

### B10. Proposition 9.3 - zero-transcript black-box verification

A deterministic exact verifier receiving a claimed zero artifact and accessing the true source only by individual value queries must query every point on the all-zero transcript. Otherwise an unqueried single-spike Boolean function is a rank-one outer product and is consistent with all observations. **CORRECT BUT STANDARD.** This does not apply to a verifier given an intensional formula, a proof certificate, authenticated compiler provenance, or stronger aggregate queries. S15 demonstrates an existing certificate-based route in knowledge compilation, outside this lower-bound model.

